[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   The velocity-selection theorem (Gate 6-I) explains the offset as a
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   selection effect: the D-search protocol selects ordinary H clouds
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   that happen to lie near the isotope-shifted position. The offset is
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   the cloud's kinematic velocity (a fitted nuisance parameter), not
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   a TEP-produced shift.
[13:07:21] [PROCESS] 
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   TEP predicts a small conformal clock shift (~0.005 km/s) on top of
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   ordinary cloud kinematics. This is 296× below
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   spectral resolution and is not directly detectable in current spectra.
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   The decisive test is the orphan population (Gate 6), which tests the
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   velocity-selection theorem's prediction of a population of ordinary
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   H clouds at non-isotope offsets.
[13:07:21] [PROCESS] 
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]   NOTE on sign vs amplitude:
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]     The clock shift Δln A is purely conformal (since ∂₀φ = 0 for a
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]     static field, the disformal term B(φ)∇₀φ∇₀φ = 0 for g₀₀).
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]     The SIGN of the clock shift is rigorous and independent of the
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]     disformal sector. The temporal-disformal channel (Gate 4B-IV)
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]     provides the dominant geometry-dependent contribution via the
[13:07:21] [PROCESS]     cosmological ∂₀φ, with fitted free-H displacements as TEP targets.
